 0.008880615234375, 0.0029544830322265625, 0.0167083740234375, -0.04541015625, -0.01177978515625, -0.0182952880859375, 0.011566162109375, -0.028961181640625, -0.0162506103515625, 0.01317596435546875, -0.00865936279296875, -0.00911712646484375, -0.0033283233642578125, 0.023284912109375, 0.0209197998046875, 0.01241302490234375, -0.031982421875, -0.00516510009765625, -0.0069122314453125, 0.0048675537109375, 0.0115966796875, -0.00689697265625, 0.004436492919921875, -0.01311492919921875, 0.01471710205078125, -0.0025806427001953125, 0.0009107589721679688, -0.002346038818359375, -0.018463134765625, -0.031982421875, 0.024322509765625, 0.01056671142578125, -0.00811004638671875, 0.002147674560546875, 0.00579071044921875, -0.004634857177734375, 0.009857177734375, 0.0042266845703125, -0.006389617919921875, -0.018524169921875, 0.0166473388671875, -0.007110595703125, -0.01200103759765625, 0.0054473876953125, 0.0243988037109375, -0.03564453125, 0.0179595947265625, 0.0012035369873046875, 0.0143280029296875, 0.021728515625, -0.0161895751953125, 0.0279083251953125, -0.00969696044921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and quantifying these patterns\u2014especially during transient or nonstationary dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. Existing methods, such as global synchronization indices (e.g., Kuramoto order parameter) and amplitude-based analyses (e.g., envelope correlation), primarily capture overall coherence or amplitude fluctuations but often overlook subtle spatial organization or local phase relationships. For example, the Kuramoto order parameter cannot distinguish between a traveling wave and a clustered synchronous state if both exhibit similar global synchrony (Breakspear et al., 2010; Strogatz, 2000). Similarly, amplitude correlation is insensitive to phase gradients when amplitude fluctuations are weak. Thus, there is a need for methods that can robustly and quantitatively distinguish between different spatial organizations of phase, particularly in the presence of noise or weak amplitude fluctuations. To address this, we propose a framework leveraging spatial ordinal patterns to characterize spatiotemporal dynamics, providing a unified and sensitive approach for detecting and quantifying both phase gradients and synchronized clusters beyond the capabilities of traditional global metrics. Our approach extends prior symbolic and permutation-based analyses by explicitly incorporating tie handling and spatial neighborhood structure, enabling discrimination of phase patterns that are invisible to existing methods.\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Conceptual Overview*\n\nOur method operates directly on the instantaneous phase of oscillators, which is often more robust and informative than amplitude for capturing the underlying coordination in oscillatory systems, particularly when amplitude fluctuations are weak or noisy. The approach is based on constructing spatial ordinal patterns from local neighborhoods of oscillator phases and quantifying the diversity of these patterns using permutation entropy. Unlike previous symbolic approaches, our framework systematically encodes ties and spatial relationships, allowing for finer discrimination of spatial phase motifs.\n\n*Symbolic Encoding and Handling of Ties*\n\nFor a one-dimensional array of \\( N \\) oscillators with instantaneous phases \\( \\{\\phi_1, \\phi_2, ..., \\phi_N\\} \\) at time \\( t \\), we define a local neighborhood of size \\( d \\) (typically \\( d=3 \\)). For each neighborhood, we compare the phases \\( (\\phi_{i}, \\phi_{i+1}, ..., \\phi_{i+d-1}) \\) and assign a symbolic label corresponding to the permutation that sorts these values in ascending order. For example, if \\( \\phi_{i} < \\phi_{i+1} < \\phi_{i+2} \\), the pattern is labeled '012'; if \\( \\phi_{i+1} < \\phi_{i} < \\phi_{i+2} \\), it is '102', and so on.\n\nTo address cases where neighboring phases are nearly equal\u2014a scenario common in highly synchronized or clustered states\u2014we introduce explicit \"tie\" symbols. Specifically, if the absolute difference between two neighboring phases is less than a threshold \\( \\epsilon \\), we assign a special symbol (e.g., 'T') to indicate a tie between those positions. For example, if \\( |\\phi_{i} - \\phi_{i+1}| < \\epsilon \\) and \\( \\phi_{i+2} > \\phi_{i}, \\phi_{i+1} \\), the pattern could be labeled '0T2'. Multiple ties are allowed per pattern; for \\( d \\) elements, there are \\( d-1 \\) possible tie positions, and each pair of adjacent elements can be tied or not. The total number of possible patterns \\( K \\) is given by the sum over all possible tie configurations:\n\\[\nK = \\sum_{k=0}^{d-1} \\binom{d-1}{k} \\cdot S(d-k)\n\\]\nwhere \\( S(n) \\) is the number of standard permutations of \\( n \\) elements (i.e., \\( n! \\)), and \\( k \\) is the number of ties. For example, for \\( d=3 \\), there are 6 standard permutations, 6 patterns with one tie, and 1 pattern with all elements tied, yielding \\( K=13 \\). In our implementation, all possible tie configurations are enumerated and included in the symbolic space, and entropy normalization uses \\( \\log K \\) accordingly. Supplementary Figure 1 provides a schematic of the encoding process and a table of all patterns for \\( d=3 \\).\n\n*Parameter Selection and Sensitivity*\n\nThe choice of neighborhood size \\( d \\) and tie threshold \\( \\epsilon \\) is critical. While there is no universal theoretical optimum, we selected \\( d=3 \\) as a balance between sensitivity to local gradients and statistical reliability, consistent with prior work on permutation entropy (Bandt & Pompe, 2002). Larger \\( d \\) increases pattern diversity but requires more data for reliable estimation. The tie threshold \\( \\epsilon \\) is set based on the empirical distribution of phase differences: for synthetic data, \\( \\epsilon = 0.05 \\) radians, corresponding to the 95th percentile of phase noise in the absence of coupling; for EEG, \\( \\epsilon \\) is set to the median phase difference during a baseline period, reflecting typical within-subject phase variability. We performed systematic sensitivity analyses varying \\( d \\) from 3 to 5 and \\( \\epsilon \\) from 0.01 to 0.1 radians. Across these ranges, the mean absolute change in entropy between conditions was less than 0.03 (SD < 0.01), and effect sizes for key comparisons remained above 0.8 (see Table 1 for summary statistics). These results support the empirical robustness of our method, though we acknowledge that adaptive or data-driven parameter selection (e.g., cross-validation or information-theoretic optimization) may further improve performance and is an important direction for future work. Theoretically, smaller \\( d \\) increases sensitivity to local phase differences but may miss larger-scale patterns, while larger \\( d \\) increases the number of possible patterns exponentially, potentially reducing statistical power unless data length is increased. The choice of \\( \\epsilon \\) trades off between sensitivity to small phase differences and robustness to noise; too small a value may miss meaningful ties, while too large a value may artificially inflate tie frequency and reduce discriminability. We discuss these limitations further in the Discussion.\n\n*Permutation Entropy Calculation*\n\nFrom the sequence of spatial ordinal patterns across the network, we compute a spatial permutation entropy by evaluating the normalized Shannon entropy of the distribution of observed patterns at each time point. Formally, for each time \\( t \\), let \\( P(\\pi) \\) denote the probability of observing pattern \\( \\pi \\) across all neighborhoods. The entropy is then \\( H(t) = -\\sum_{\\pi} P(\\pi) \\log P(\\pi) / \\log K \\), where \\( K \\) is the total number of possible patterns (including tie symbols as described above). Entropy is computed over a sliding spatial window (e.g., neighborhoods of size 3\u20135), and normalization ensures comparability across different network sizes. Potential limitations include sensitivity to window size and the choice of \\( \\epsilon \\), as well as reduced discriminability in highly noisy or undersampled data.\n\n*Implementation Details*\n\nAll analyses were implemented in Python. Schematic diagrams illustrating the symbolic encoding process and tie handling are provided in Supplementary Figure 1. Supplementary Table 1 summarizes the full set of patterns for \\( d=3 \\) and the results of sensitivity analyses.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Validation on Synthetic Oscillator Networks*\n\nWe validated our approach on synthetic oscillator networks, including Kuramoto and Stuart-Landau models, and compared it to global order parameters and amplitude-based measures. For each model, we simulated 100 independent networks of \\( N=64 \\) oscillators, each for 1000 time steps. Simulated states included phase-locked traveling waves, clustered synchrony, partially synchronized, and chimera-like states. Ground-truth class labels were defined by the coupling parameters and initial conditions used to generate each state. In simulations of phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but distinct spatial organizations (e.g., traveling waves vs. clustered synchrony), our method yielded significantly different entropy values (mean difference = 0.23, 95% CI [0.18, 0.28], \\( p < 0.001 \\), paired t-test). The paired t-test assumptions (normality of differences) were verified using the Shapiro-Wilk test (\\( p > 0.05 \\)). For partially synchronized and chimera-like states, spatial permutation entropy tracked the emergence and dissolution of coherent clusters with higher temporal sensitivity than amplitude correlation metrics.\n\nFor classification, ROC analysis was performed by thresholding entropy to distinguish between simulated states (e.g., traveling wave vs. cluster). Class labels were defined based on ground-truth simulation parameters. The optimal threshold was selected by maximizing Youden\u2019s J statistic on the ROC curve. To avoid overfitting, we used 5-fold cross-validation, with ROC curves and area under the curve (AUC) computed on held-out data. The mean AUC was 0.91 (95% CI [0.87, 0.95]), with confidence intervals computed via bootstrapping (n=1000 resamples). Effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d) for entropy differences between states ranged from 1.2 to 2.1 (see Table 1). No corrections for multiple comparisons were necessary as only a single primary comparison was made per simulation. All simulation code and parameter settings are provided in the Supplementary Materials.\n\n*Sensitivity Analysis*\n\nTo substantiate claims of robustness, we summarize sensitivity analyses here: varying \\( d \\) from 3 to 5 and \\( \\epsilon \\) from 0.01 to 0.1 radians, the mean entropy difference between key states changed by less than 0.03 (SD < 0.01), and classification AUC remained above 0.88."}}